 for that is  the tight association of dna with  histone so there is  probably a lot more cross-linked protein  than  in case of rna and then the third steps  because we still have  the protein residues cross-linked to the  nucleic acid after the proteinase  treatment  we need some way of removing those or  getting rid of  as much or as many of those as possible  to make  the nucleic acid a good substrate for  enzymatic reactions  and this is usually done by heat  treatment in optimized buffers that  helps chemically  breaking these crosslinks  and for that step also  there is a difference between dna and  rna in that  dna requires a harsher treatment  than rna and at the same time the dna  can also tolerate a harsher treatment  whereas  rna you have to be more careful  that if the temperature is too high or  the treatment too long it may cause  additional fragmentation of the nucleic  acid i will now  go through each of these important steps  and provide some data to illustrate  important aspects and what we consider  best  starting with the paraffinization  so there are certain protocols where the  paraffinization is done by  simple melting of the paraffin in lysis  buffer  and here in this uh experiment this is  compared  to the paraffinization using our uh  departurization  solution this is done in k here for dna  extraction  and dna yield has been determined  by uv-vis-based quantitation like a  nano-drop  and also using a fluorescent dye based  essay  what you can see immediately is that  uv this od measurement tends to  overestimate  yields quite a bit this is something  that we are seeing  uh systematically and particularly in  case of ffpe  samples so you need to be careful  uh and mindful of what the methods you  use  and how it compares to other methods  and what you can also see that for both  methods  we're getting quite a bit higher yields  if we use the departurization solution  to really separate  the paraffin efficiently  compared to just melting  and we also did qpcr here  for um the amplicon is a  short piece of ribosomal dna from the  18s  gene um and what you can see  basically the pcr results confirms uh  the  quantitation the physical quantitation  in that we see quite a bit lower  ct values when we use the  paraffinization solution  here we are looking at isolubilization  of rna from ffpe sections  and what is the actual time required for  efficient solubilization of the rna  specifically  and what you can see here so the time  frame or the digestion times used here  there from 15 minutes  up to 24 hours of protein sk digestion  the small picture here shows that  in a lot of cases you need more than  three hours for  complete digestion of pieces of tissue  and for optimal rna  yield you can see that 15 minutes or 24  hours days  in all cases for all of these tissues  there is actually very little difference  indicating that 15 minutes is enough  and you do not need full solubilization  of the tissue  and the same is also con confirmed when  instead of  physical rna yield determination we're  looking at  qpcr we get  there is a lot of variability here which  is typical for  ffpe samples but essentially we're  getting the same ct values after 15  minutes that we get after 24 hours  on the other hand what you can see on  the right  looking at the bioanalyzer electro  ferrograms  that after prolonged protein sk  treatment 24 hours you start actually  seeing a negative effect on  rna integrity so not only are 15 minutes  sufficient it's also better for your rna  quality  based on the observation that rna and  dna  require different times to solubilize  from ffpe  section scientists said kyogen actually  developed a protocol for  isolation of or separate isolation of  dna and rna  from the same ffpe section  without splitting samples or anything  and the way that works is after the  paraffinization a short  protein sk treatment is performed to  solubilize the rna  and then that first lysate is removed  and the remaining tissue is  treated with additional protein sk for a  longer time  so you have a second lysate that then  and and then you can isolate the rna  from the first lysate and the dna from  the second lysate  and importantly this patented method  also allows  to use in the ensuing steps optimal  conditions  for crosslink removal as well so we can  do  a more gentle crosslink removal for the  rna and a much more stringent one for  the dna  with so we get really optimal results  for each of the two analytes  and of course the benefit of getting  both  nucleic acids from the same section is a  big benefit  not only because you get higher yields  than by  splitting the samples in half but you  also get  the analytes from the exact same cell  population so  if you have for example a tissue section  that contains both normal and  tumor cells this will ensure that  the portion of tumor cells that you  isolate  your nucleic acid from is the same  exactly same from  for both analytes here is some data  showing why the crosslink removal step  is so crucial for  nucleic acid isolation from ffpe in this  case  for rna isolation on the left you can  see  yields determined by od measurement  for both lung and kidney tissue  and for different first we have  different  times for of pk treatment 15 minutes at  55 degrees  and also 30 minutes and then  for each of these two protein sk  treatment times we  also have additional heat incubation  at 80 degrees for either 10 minutes or  20 minutes  and this is again in a buffer that is  specifically optimized to break  such crosslinks and if you look at the  yields  you can see that at 30 minutes  compared to 15 minutes you get quite a  bit higher  yield especially from kidney and  when you do only the 15 minutes you get  you reach the same yield if you include  the 80 degree incubation on the right  you then can see how this  isolated rna performs in qpcr  and important here is we normalized the  rna input into each sa  to 50 nanogram and  again for the 15 minutes 55 degree  without the actual 80 degree crosslink  removal step  you can see if it doesn't matter if we  do 15 or 30 minutes at 55 degree the ct  values are roughly the same  and when we include the crosslink  removal step then we get a dramatic  improvement of the pcr results the ct  value actually drops  from around 28 to  less than 24. so this is a  huge difference and shows why the  crosslink removal is  really essential here or in general for  all  ffpe centers and the same is true  for dna isolation as well  in this experiment there are different  crosslink removal times and conditions  you can see starting from 30 minutes at  90 degree  up to 30 90 degree 30 minutes or one  hour  and then compared to 70 or 80 degrees  for one hour or for four hours  and also compared to just a prolonged  protein ace k  treatment overnight at 55 or 56 degrees  centigrade  and again on the left is the  a dna quantitation sorry this is dna of  course  and you can see the highest yield  we get with the one hour at 90 degree  and with the prolonged  incubation at 56 degree we can in some  cases get almost as good  dna in other cases not so there is a lot  more variability  but what is important when you look  again at the qpcr results  on the right and again here the dna  input is normalized  you can see that in this case the  56 degree incubation is not  good enough even if you do it overnight  and the best results really by far  are those obtained after one hour at 90  degrees  and almo pretty much the same at  four hours for four hours at 80 degrees  but anything less than that lower  temperatures or shorter incubation times  lead to poor performance in pcr  and in this case for both  long and a short amplicon 66 and 500  base pairs  again from the 18s ribosomal dna  gene another important complication  from ffpe samples is that  the formaldehyde fixation and storage  can result in deamination of cytosines  and this would eventually  result in artificial c2t transitions  that can then show up as false  false positives or false variant  especially when you do sequencing  of of these dna molecules  and to exclude those artificial  mutations from from your analysis the  best way is really to do a  ung treatment uracil and glycosylase  this will cut out the  deaminated cytosine so the aminated  cytosine is uracil  and the enzyme removes that and so those  affected dna strands are not included in  your sequencing reactions and will not  give  false false mutations at the end  so you can see that on the left the  number of mutations  that are present at very low frequencies  and you can see that the ung treatment  removes those mutations  and then those mutations that are  reported that the higher frequency the  true mutations  they are maintained and um  this you can see on the right here these  are uh  the mutations the true mutations and  those  are not affected by by the enzymatic  treatment  we're now moving to quality control of  the isolated nucleic acids  and for dna one aspect  that people are looking at is the  integrity or  physical length of the isolated dna  and here you can see two examples from  actually kyogen kit used for  dna isolation one is the chiamp dna ffpe  tissue kit  and the other the generate dna ffpe kit  and what you can see easily is that  the generate the dna isolate with the  gene read kit is a little bit shorter  than with the kaya amp kit but so you  would normally say  that the dna quality is maybe worse  but in reality when you look at pcr  performance  here again two different uh length  amplicons from the  uh human prion protein gene 78  and 465 base pair amplicon lengths  respectively  you can see that actually the generate  the dna isolated using the generate kit  gives a  better ct value even so the  dna looks less intact on the gel  and uh so that is true for qpcr  the that the integrity is not really  a main indicator in part  because uh short dna often performs  better in qpcr  and also because the physical length of  the dna  does not reflect to what degree the  crosslinks are  actually reversed and with respect to  sequencing  uh since we are looking at a 465 base  per amplicon here  you can also probably uh agree with me  that  this is easily long enough for all the  standard ngs application or library  preparation protocols  so this is not really limiting in any  way  in case of rna integrity is usually  assessed using a capillary  electrophoresis  with instruments like the agilent  bioanalyzer or  the kaya xl from kyogen and those  instruments often provide something like  an rna  integrity number in this case here shown  again for the bioanalyzer  for a different rna  samples here rna was isolated  from red tissue sections either  shortly after fixation and embedding  this is the t0 on the left and  also after 12 months of storage at  either 4 degree  room temperature or 37 degree and what  you can see  immediately is that the rna integrity is  very different for all of these but  it's also different for the room  temperature and 37 degrees  but the rim numbers are actually fairly  close together  so they in our few do not really  uh fully uh represent the quality  difference like in for example for the  brain tissue  after 12 months at room temperature you  can still see  a bit of ribosome of a ribosomal peak  here  and for the 37 degree sample  it's much more degraded but the rin  number is actually not dramatically  different  so in our mind or in our view  for real numbers below like four or so  they do not provide that much  information anymore  or much differentiation and apparently  people at illumina also came to the same  conclusion  so they suggested uh an alternative  parameter for rna integrity  for especially for sequencing which they  called dv200  which i will comment on in the next  slide  dv 200 is quite simply  the portion of the fluorescent signal  that comes from  rna fragments longer than 209 200  nucleotide  versus those from shorter  fragments and the rationality behind  that is obviously that  rna fragments shorter than 200  nucleotides are considered  not very useful as at least for long rna  sequencing  and the purpose of such a measure is to  compare different rna samples with  regard to preservation of  rna integrity and what kind of  sequencing results can be expected  from those what is important  to keep in mind is first  the electropherogram shows the physical  integrity  but it does not reflect the chemical  modification by formaldehyde the  crosslinks that may be remaining or  remnants of the crosslink proteins that  will interfere  with cdna synthesis or pcr amplification  and to include that aspect of nucleic  acid quality  the best way is really to perform qpcr  on decembers  and the other thing that is important to  keep in mind  um that this parameter is  not really suitable to compare different  rna  isolation methods and how well they  preserve rna  integrity because different methods and  protocols  often differ in how well they  isolate short rna fragments so some  methods that do not isolate any rna  below 200 nucleotides will  pretty much always give high dv 200  even if the sample is not great the  yield will be  just drop a lot so that is  important to keep in mind  to summarize that aspect nucleic acid  integrity or length distribution  is important but it also depends on the  downstream application that  the nucleic acid is used in for ngs  based analysis  often you need a minimum length of maybe  200 base pair or longer  whereas for pcr and qpcr short  fragments can still be analyzed and and  also provide really useful information  and in many cases when you have a kit or  protocol  that isolates short fragments like you  see here in the example on the right  this is a pretty old  data piece of data  it's actually quite easy to modify that  protocol  to not recover these short fragments  in case they are really unwanted  and before i c